===persian===

هذیان بعد از جنگ...
اگر ابرها خاکستری نبودند
باران رنگ دیگری داشت
و اشک‌های تو
سبز کم رنگ بودند.
رنگ جنگ در آه می‌ماند در نگاه می‌ماند
من نفرت از جنگ را در چشم‌ها دیده‌ام
و نفرت از صلح را در دندان‌ها
اگر ابرها خاکستری نبودند
باران رنگ دیگری داشت
می‌دانم چشم‌هایت در مهتاب زیباترند
اما...
باید به پناهگاه برگردیم

تهران ۱۳۶۸

===machine===

Delirium after war...
If the clouds were not gray
The rain would have another color
And your tears
Would be pale green.
The color of war remains in a sigh, remains in a gaze
I have seen the hatred of war in eyes
And the hatred of peace in teeth
If the clouds were not gray
The rain would have another color
I know your eyes are more beautiful in the moonlight
But...
We must return to the shelter

Tehran 1368

===lantern===

Delirium after the war...
If the clouds were not grey
the rain would wear another colour
and your tears
would be a pale green.
The colour of war lingers in a sigh, lingers in a gaze.
I have seen the hatred of war in people's eyes,
and the hatred of peace in their teeth.
If the clouds were not grey
the rain would wear another colour.
I know your eyes are lovelier in the moonlight,
but...
we must go back to the shelter.

Tehran 1368

===translation===

Delirium after the war...
If the clouds were not grey,
the rain would take another colour,
and your tears
would be a faint green.
The colour of war stays in a sigh, stays in a gaze.
I have seen the hatred of war in their eyes,
and the hatred of peace in their teeth.
If the clouds were not grey,
the rain would take another colour.
I know your eyes are more beautiful in the moonlight —
but...
we have to go back to the shelter.

— Tehran, 1368

===footnotes===

هذیان is the raving of fever or shell-shock — "delirium." The sharpest line is the symmetry of نفرت از جنگ ... و نفرت از صلح: hatred of war seen in the eyes, hatred of peace bared in the teeth — a people sickened by war yet unable to be at peace. The colour logic (grey cloud → grey rain → drained tears) is the same drained palette as the page-67 painter's poem; here the longed-for beauty of the moonlit face is cut short by the return to the bomb-shelter (پناهگاه), the same shelter as the page-60 poem.
